WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.440
- All right y'all, we are back

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.990
and we've got Seth Rogen and
Jessica Williams with us.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:04.871
Give it up.

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:06.120
(applause)

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.640
So our next guest is
pretty much the definition

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.900
of an empowered woman.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.820
She is an attorney, activist

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.670
and a number one New York
Times bestselling author

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:15.960
'cause she didn't have enough jobs.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.397
Her latest project is a
children's book called

00:00:18.397 --> 00:00:19.890
"A is for Ambitious."

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.111
Please welcome Meena Harris, everybody.

00:00:22.111 --> 00:00:23.705
(applause)

00:00:23.705 --> 00:00:24.538
- Did you--
- Hi!

00:00:24.538 --> 00:00:26.580
- Did you know Seth or did
you know Jessica before?

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:27.936
- No. We've just met for
the first time today.

00:00:27.936 --> 00:00:28.769
- Hi.

00:00:28.769 --> 00:00:29.730
- I can't believe I'm the only,

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:30.570
apparently I learned,

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.360
the only one who's not a ceramist.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.130
- No.
- I didn't get the memo.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:36.170
- Oh, I've never, I've never done it.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.185
- [Meena] Or ceramicist?
- Yeah! (laughs)

00:00:38.185 --> 00:00:40.020
- I would want to, I would love to do it.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.146
- [Meena] Right?

00:00:41.146 --> 00:00:42.813
- But I've never, it seems
very hard, but I would-

00:00:42.813 --> 00:00:43.646
- It's pretty hard.

00:00:43.646 --> 00:00:46.350
- Yeah, it really is but once
you start getting those pulls

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.730
- And it's like therapeutic right?

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:48.836
- It is yeah.
- Very therapeutic.

00:00:48.836 --> 00:00:50.044
- You like really you get to focus.

00:00:50.044 --> 00:00:50.877
- Very, very. Yeah.

00:00:50.877 --> 00:00:52.238
- I think I'll just focus still on songs.

00:00:52.238 --> 00:00:53.333
(guests laugh)

00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:54.390
So I was like, I think
that'll take me forever,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.223
but I'd love to learn.

00:00:55.223 --> 00:00:56.490
But congrats on the new book.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:57.420
This is,
- Thank you so much.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.010
- kind of a sequel of sorts, right?

00:00:59.010 --> 00:00:59.843
- It is.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:59.843 --> 00:01:01.290
- So the first book that came-

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.615
this is actually my fourth kid's book,

00:01:02.615 --> 00:01:03.448
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:03.448 --> 00:01:04.770
- which I never thought I would say.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:07.140
It is the sequel to "Ambitious Girl,"

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.400
which is right there.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.180
And "Ambitious Girl" is really
about the word "ambitious"

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.850
and ambition and the way
in which we as a society

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.790
often use that word against
women and girls, and

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:18.623
- Yeah.

00:01:18.623 --> 00:01:19.557
- It's about the power of language

00:01:19.557 --> 00:01:22.110
and really reclaiming and
redefining these words

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.802
on our own terms. And-

00:01:23.802 --> 00:01:25.350
- It's just so important
because ambitious,

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.540
and being ambitious,
is such a great thing.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.430
But you're right, it can
be used so negatively.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.860
- Exactly. It's a good thing.
I was raised to believe

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:32.693
that it was a good thing.

00:01:32.693 --> 00:01:33.660
It's something to be proud of,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.870
but as we all know,
society often tells women,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.580
women of color, you know, hide that.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.040
You can be ambitious but
not too ambitious, right?

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:41.873
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:41.873 --> 00:01:42.810
Let's stay in your, stay in your lane.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.100
So that's what the first book was about,

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.470
and while I was writing
it and then promoting it,

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:48.960
I was joking that I could probably come up

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.460
with a word for every letter

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.660
of the alphabet that
people use against women

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:53.580
- Yeah.
- and girls.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:54.840
And I was like, wait,
that's actually a good idea.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:55.950
I'm gonna write an ABC book.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.200
So that's what "A is for Ambitious"

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.100
- is all about.
- So cool.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.210
- Well I have an eight
year old little girl

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.500
and I love that you wrote this 'cause

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.430
this is very important.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:03.263
- [Meena] It really is.

00:02:03.263 --> 00:02:04.410
- To get to instill this kind of stuff

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:05.243
'cause they-

00:02:05.243 --> 00:02:07.530
Words matter. They're so powerful.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:08.460
- And doing it early.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:09.293
Early on, right?
- [Kelly] Yeah!

00:02:09.293 --> 00:02:10.860
- In our homes, using the power of

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:11.790
- children's literature
- [Kelly] Yes!

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.110
- to embolden them and empower them

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.460
at a young age is so important.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:15.293
Because you're right.

00:02:15.293 --> 00:02:16.500
Like I grew up in the south

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.270
and that's where it's like, I mean,

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:19.500
be bold, but not that bold.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:20.333
You know what I'm saying?
- [Meena] Right.

00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:21.870
Like, don't outshine the boys.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:22.703
Like, you know,
- Exactly.

00:02:22.703 --> 00:02:23.536
that kind of thing.

00:02:23.536 --> 00:02:25.260
No offense, I love boys, but-

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.581
- Ouch. Please, outshine away!

00:02:27.581 --> 00:02:28.680
(guests laughing)

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.140
- So, Meena, you were
raised to be empowered.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:31.973
Like you were raised

00:02:31.973 --> 00:02:33.570
around pretty powerful women in general.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:34.403
- I was, I grew up-

00:02:34.403 --> 00:02:35.739
- [Kelly] I did some research
on you and your family.

00:02:35.739 --> 00:02:36.572
- Oh!

00:02:36.572 --> 00:02:37.405
- Yeah!

00:02:37.405 --> 00:02:39.330
- Well, I-I did grow up in a

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.850
small family that was unique in that

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.230
it was all women.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:46.560
It was me, my mother, my
grandmother, who was really

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.630
a second mother to me and my aunt.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.030
And I joke that it was
like the opening scene

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.200
of the Wonder Woman movie,
- [Kelly] (laughs)

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.850
like these incredible women of color,

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.994
just like running around,
saving the world, and-

00:02:55.994 --> 00:02:56.827
- [Kelly] With incredible
jobs, like- yeah.

00:02:56.827 --> 00:02:57.660
Really making-

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:58.920
- And all, you know, in service

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.530
and doing really, you
know, had purpose, right?

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:01.363
- Yeah.

00:03:01.363 --> 00:03:03.030
- And now as a parent, I appreciate

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.130
that even more as an adult,
I appreciate that more,

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:05.963
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:05.963 --> 00:03:06.870
- just how formative that was for me.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:07.950
And I think about, you know,

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.000
how I'm raising my own children.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.370
Not only how I can carry
that legacy forward

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:15.060
but also that my kids in-in
many good ways are growing

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.160
up in a different, you know, environment.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.667
We let a few men in along the way,

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:19.664
[Kelly] (laughs)

00:03:19.664 --> 00:03:20.497
so there's that, but you know

00:03:20.497 --> 00:03:21.924
- To, you know, have the kids.

00:03:21.924 --> 00:03:22.757
(laughing)

00:03:22.757 --> 00:03:23.590
- Yeah, exactly.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.200
- That, that, that's how that works.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.033
And you know, just thinking

00:03:26.033 --> 00:03:27.390
about parenting in my own way, right?

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:30.150
But so honoring what
was so formative for me

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:31.200
but really carrying that forward.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.910
And it's ultimately,
in terms of the books,

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.470
the message is really that, you know,

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.840
we can do all the like girl power, rah rah

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:37.860
girl power stuff at home

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.600
but we know that when girls step out

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.220
into the world, it's-it's different.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.160
- Is there a word or a
characteristic that you kind of

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.200
proudly represent that's
been, like, here's my thing:

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.152
I always wanted to be a
diva whenever I was a kid.

00:03:48.152 --> 00:03:48.985
- Oh.

00:03:48.985 --> 00:03:49.890
- I mean, that was Aretha Franklin,

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:50.893
- [Women] Yes!

00:03:50.893 --> 00:03:52.320
that was Whitney, that was
Mariah, that was Celine.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.290
It was all these amazing
women that I loved.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.810
And then when I- by the
time I got to the industry,

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.520
it was like, that was a horrible,

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.350
shameful word to be called.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:02.880
- Oh we should have put diva for "D"!

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:03.713
- No, I just-

00:04:03.713 --> 00:04:04.710
- There's so many words, right?

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.061
- No, I know, there's so many words.

00:04:06.061 --> 00:04:07.327
- Got another book?
- but it really-

00:04:07.327 --> 00:04:08.864
(laughing)
- it-it broke-

00:04:08.864 --> 00:04:10.200
(laughing) it broke-

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.130
- It broke my heart, though,

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:11.963
'cause I was like, oh man.

00:04:11.963 --> 00:04:13.560
I was like, that was
like my goal as a child.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:14.970
I was like, I'm gonna be
a diva when I'm older.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:15.803
- [Meena] Right.

00:04:15.803 --> 00:04:17.850
- You know, 'cause it-it,
you know, started with like,

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.520
you know, even classical
singers that were,

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:21.540
you know, these big operatic,
- Sure.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:22.890
like, first sopranos that were just like,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:24.240
oh my god, you know, a diva.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.580
Like, and it cha- we've altered that name

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:27.413
- [Meena] Exactly.

00:04:27.413 --> 00:04:28.740
- and, and it kind of sucks for me.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:29.621
That's a word I love.

00:04:29.621 --> 00:04:30.720
- And you know, it's like, it's also too

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.700
because diva is very much associated

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:33.900
with, with women.
- [Meena] Right.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:35.640
It's one of those things- diva you think

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.067
of someone who's demanding and asking

00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:39.840
for the best or what they
feel like they deserve.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:40.844
- Yeah.

00:04:40.844 --> 00:04:43.830
- And so diva is like, oh,
she's asking for too much.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:47.130
When really it's like, somebody
probably treated that diva

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.080
like trash a lot of the time.
- (agreement)

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.880
- So she's like, yeah, I'm a diva.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.320
I have to be this way.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:53.400
Because you, you're trying me.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.320
- So I like that "D" and I like diva.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:56.153
- Yeah.
- [Meena] Yeah.

00:04:56.153 --> 00:04:57.210
- I gonna do-
- [Kelly] Do you-?

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.260
- Yeah, I have one.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.630
I have "B," "B" black.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.180
Just being black and what's, you know,

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.280
identity and I think a
lot of times growing up

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.660
people of color or a woman,
your identity is associated

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.970
with things that are both,
you know, bad and good

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.963
and it depends on where you
live and where you grew up.

00:05:13.963 --> 00:05:14.796
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:14.796 --> 00:05:16.410
- And how you internalize being black.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:20.010
So "B" for black and
then probably like "T"

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:22.140
for trans and like trans lives matter

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.940
and protect trans people at all costs.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:24.773
- Yeah.

00:05:24.773 --> 00:05:25.680
- All of that, because it's like

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.060
identity and racial politics.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:27.893
- [Meena] Yep.

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:28.726
- It's really important.

00:05:28.726 --> 00:05:29.640
- Absolutely.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.110
Do you have a word?
(applause)

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:32.097
You're like, he's like, I wanna go!

00:05:32.097 --> 00:05:34.363
- Not as good as theirs. (laughs)

00:05:34.363 --> 00:05:35.818
- [Meena] I know he has a word!

00:05:35.818 --> 00:05:37.509
- He's like, I'm out! I'm out!

00:05:37.509 --> 00:05:39.429
- I'll let them take this one. (laughs)

00:05:39.429 --> 00:05:41.670
- I thought your word, Seth,
would be the letter "H."

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:42.528
- For "hi?"

00:05:42.528 --> 00:05:44.682
(laughter)

00:05:44.682 --> 00:05:45.600
- No, hot!
(laughter)

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.168
- I would never have thought "hi."

00:05:47.168 --> 00:05:49.080
(laughter and applause)

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:49.913
- Oh my god.

00:05:49.913 --> 00:05:52.650
Meena's new book is called
"A is for Ambitious".

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.720
Pick it up wherever you
get your books, everybody.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.820
We always suggest a small bookstore.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:58.020
All right? Shop locally.

